<!--
  The `tools` namespace is deliberately absent. It was declared solely for a
  `tools:overrideLibrary` on Health Connect, and removing the declaration makes
  reintroducing that override a visible two-line change rather than a one-word
  one.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  package="com.projectstride.stride_health">

  <!--
    Project Stride's minimum is API 26, everywhere.

    There is deliberately NO `tools:overrideLibrary` here. Health Connect's
    client library declares minSdk 26, and an override would have been the
    build system asserting, on our behalf, that the SDK supports API 24-25.
    It does not, and saying so in a manifest does not make it so — it only
    moves the failure from build time to a stranger's phone.

    The API bands, which `HealthConnectStepSource.availability()` implements
    and `HealthConnectAvailabilityTest` asserts:

      24-25  unsupported. The app does not install.
      26-27  installs; Health Connect reports UNAVAILABLE. No permission is
             requested and nothing crashes — the same normal, fully-playable
             state as a phone without Health Connect installed.
      28+    normal availability checking via `getSdkStatus`.

    The 26-27 band exists because the client library is loadable there while
    the Health Connect platform is not present, so the honest answer is
    "unavailable", reached without touching a Health Connect type.
  -->

  <!--
    Read-only, steps only.

    There is no write permission here and there must not be one: the game reads
    the player's movement and never writes to their health record. There is also
    no history permission - the ledger never needs old data, only what is new
    since the last cursor, and asking for more than that is asking the player to
    grant access the game cannot justify.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_STEPS" />

  <!--
    Package visibility for the Health Connect provider.

    Without this, `HealthConnectClient.getSdkStatus` cannot see the provider on
    Android 11 through 13 and reports it missing on a device that has it
    installed. Absence is a normal state the game stays fully playable through,
    which is exactly why a FALSE absence would be invisible.
  -->
  <queries>
    <package android:name="com.google.android.apps.healthdata" />
  </queries>

  <!--
    Deliberately absent: any background entry point.

    No service, no receiver, no `PassiveMonitoringClient` registration, no
    worker, no boot receiver. S-01A is foreground synchronization only;
    background delivery is S-01B and is blocked on a real persistence
    coordinator. See DECISIONS/0013 and DECISIONS/0014.

    Also deliberately absent: the permission-rationale activity Health Connect
    requires before release. It belongs to the host application, not to this
    plugin, because it is a screen the player sees and this package owns no UI.
  -->
</manifest>
